Date: Tue, 16 Nov 93 15:04:24 PST
From: Robert Brooks <rb@hprpcd.rose.hp.com>
Subject: HUMOR/INVENT: Bungee casting

I decided to go ahead and post the humorous article I've been sitting on,
because I've convinced myself that it's not just funny but might actually
launch an interesting and useful Extropian discussion.  That is, "How does
an inventor go about commercializing his/her invention?"

What would the next step be?  Building a model?  Simulation?

What about patents?  Should those of us who are philosophically opposed to
patents seek them anyway, since we live in a world where patents, like it
or not, exist?  Does this article constitute disclosure, thus foreclosing
the possibility of acquiring non-USA patents?  What effect might this notice
have:

(This is a private communication to the Extropians mailing list and does not
constitute public disclosure.  Do not forward, quote, or distribute without
permission of the author.  All list members originally receiving this message
and abiding by this restriction shall be eligible for a minority interest in
any resulting enterprise formed for the purpose of developing, manufacturing,
promoting, or marketing this invention not less than a one thirtieth of one
percent share each.)

How does one attract investors?  What is the minimum necessary to present
to them?  A model or simulation plus a business plan?  How extensive?  Or
would a full-scale working prototype be necessary?

Anyway, here goes:

(Reuters) LONDON - Patent applications were filed in major international
capitals today on a device that promises to give thrill-seekers a new sport.
The invention is particularly notable in that it represents a marriage of
medieval and 20th-century technologies.

Dubbed "Bungee casting" by the inventor, Dr. Whelan Dowd of Boston, the
envisioned sport adds a dramatic horizontal element to the vertical thrills
of Bungee jumping.  From the shore of an ocean or lake, the participant is
launched several hundred feet over the water, and then reeled back in.

The device is essentially a modern implementation of the medieval siege
engine known as a "trebuchet," to which a reel of Bungee cord is added.
Dr. Dowd, a medical researcher by day and inventor by night, explains:

"What spawned the idea was a discussion of the trebuchet on an Internet
mailing list that I'm on.  A trebuchet has a tapered arm with a pivot near
one end, supported by a frame...

"This guy in England actually built one, and has used it to fling pianos,
dead pigs, small cars, and so forth.  A stunt parachutist figured he could
get high enought to come down safely, but the acceleration on takeoff would
be so great as to be probably fatal.

"But that could be fixed by using Bungee cord for the sling.  Then the
resemblance to a gigantic fishing rod struck me, and the idea of dispensing
with the need for the parachute by doing it over water.

"I expect it to be especially popular with surfers.  Much quicker than
paddling out, and you get a thrill in both directions."


---
Robert

