Date: Tue, 16 Nov 1993 16:57:35 -0800
From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
Subject: 

So, Hal Finney is gone.  I'll be going shortly after submitting this
message.  This is not, as it turns out, an isolated phenomenon.

I've spent the last couple of days going through old mail, in an effort to
decide for myself whether the aggravation I experience due to my
subscription to this list is balanced by the level of information I derive
from it.  I'm quite impressed at the names I no longer see.

Where, I wonder, is Scott DeLancey?  Or Mark Read Pickens?  Or Han Huang? 
Or Ronald Hale-Evans?  Or Rebecca Crowley?  Or Mark McFadden?  Or Elizabeth
Schwartz?  Seems as though a number of people have either been driven off
the list, or are disinclined to participate any further.

Many months ago, I had some discussion with Harry over my unhappiness at
the direction the list was taking.  At the time, I expressed my feeling
that Extropians would soon devolve into a little clique of True Believers,
whirling furiously in ever-diminishing circles, discussing
ever-less-significant minutiae of Extropianically-correct subjects.

Harry's response amounted to asking what my point was.

Curious juxtapositions: I found a posting in which the writer chortled over
a group of witches protesting the movie "Hocus Pocus" because it failed to
portray witches accurately, as well as another posting lamenting the
treatment of cryonic suspension in the movie "Forever Young".  A foolish
consistency and all that, I guess.

My most immediate problem is what I see as a serious level on cognitive
dissonance on the list.  Extropians, it seems, do not practice what they
preach, at least not within the context of the list.  We have ostensible
neophiles who are unwilling to listen to criticism.  We have anarchists who
exhort others to do what they say or be banished.  "List law", ostensibly a
model PPL, is applied in a thoroughly arbitrary and gratuitous manner.

I once commented that it was unclear what some members of this list would
do with the "Jupiter-sized brains" they aspire to be one day, other than
act out their neuroses millions of times faster than they currently could. 
This comment still stands.  I literally shudder to think of the
consequences should some members of the list achieve their desires to wield
"godlike powers".  Handing out whiskey and car keys to teen-age boys looks
real good by comparison.

When I first joined, it seeed as though the Extropians list had a lot to
offer.  It has mostly failed, in my view, to live up to that promise.  Why
has it failed?  In my opinion, the answer is arrogance, in a variety of
forms.  Most often, it takes the shape of inappropriate rationalism,
scientism, or simple willful ignorance.  I use the word "ignorance" here in
the Buddhist sense: not simply passive not-knowing, but active
not-wanting-to-know.

At the turn of the century, it was widely believed that the study of
physics was virtually over and done with, that all that remained was the
nailing down of a few final decimal points.  Of course, that particular
opinion turned out to be radically in error.  I am therefore surprised to
see essentially the same idea surfacing on Extropians; I expect that
reality will once again prove to be disappointingly slippery.

I once asked a prominent member of the list how he could possibly expect to
"solve" what appeared to me to be a combinatorially-exploding, NP-complex
problem.  He replied, "We're committed to doing just that."  I find it
difficult to imagine how gritting one's teeth and girding one's loins will
make a dent in such a problem, but apparently Extropians don't need to
worry about such niceties.

A small number of members of the list seem to spend an inordinate amount of
time doing whatever they can to drive away others.  I can't even begin to
speculate on the reasons for this, but it seems to be the case nonetheless.
 A classic example of this occurred when one member made a statement on the
subject of Our Friend the Bonobo and was treated, in return, to a flame-
and misinformation-filled response, again from a prominent member of the
list, apparently for no particular reason other than idle hands being the
Devil's tools.  I shouldn't need to point out that this sort of approach is
a sub-optimal method of outreach.

I've been gratified by the many pieces of email I've received asking that I
stick around.  I thank those who sent them for the thought, but have to say
that I have better uses for the energy I've been expending here.  I'll
still be at <lefty@apple.com>.  Drop me a line from time to time.

