(Oh horrors! "Scientism", "Engineeritis", "Hyperrationalism") This
list describes itself as being oriented towards neophiles,  so many of the
list members are very science/technically oriented -- its perfectly
reasonable that rationalism is a standard of judgement. It's irrevelent
whether some people seem to think that yogic, buddhist, socialist, and
deathist arguments need to be heard.

> At the turn of the century, it was widely believed that the study of
> physics was virtually over and done with, that all that remained was the
> nailing down of a few final decimal points.  Of course, that particular
> opinion turned out to be radically in error.  I am therefore surprised to
> see essentially the same idea surfacing on Extropians; I expect that
> reality will once again prove to be disappointingly slippery.

   Tim May has made some excellent analogies. The "easy pickin's"
have already been found. New and more interesting fundamental physics
are going to be harder and harder to find.  Relativity and Quantum Mechanics
appeared when we gained the technology to explore previously unknown
frontiers: the very small, the very large, and the very fast. The modern
day GUT/TOE theories require energies so high it makes the super
collider look like a game of marbles making any potential new effects
outside of any entrepreneur's reach. The super collider and Hubble are
the biggest microscope and telescope we have and they cost billions and
return little new fundamental results worth exploiting.

   I doubt new colliders or the hubble will succeed in giving us FTL or
free energy.


